        "problem": "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29=0.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+8x]+[{y}^{2}-2y]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+[(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-16]+[(y-1)^{2}-1]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =32+1-29 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,1).$",
